From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU1 interpreter: skip pipeline scans when nothing is due

What broke:
  Nothing is wrong, but slow: in Ben 10: Protector of Earth's first level
  about half of the game thread's time was in the VU1 interpreter
  (44 of 60 gdb stack samples had VU1Interpreter frames). The top frames
  were commitReadyPipelines and the cycle loop that drains XGKICK after a
  microprogram ends (flushPipelines -> advanceOneCycle -> progressXgkick).

Why:
  commitReadyPipelines walks every pipeline array (flags, FDIV, EFU,
  stores, VF/VI/ACC writes: about 50 entries) on every simulated cycle,
  even when no entry is due. flushPipelines steps one cycle at a time
  until PATH1 has copied the whole XGKICK packet (2 cycles per qword),
  paying that scan and pipelinesPending's scan for each cycle, and
  progressXgkick copied each qword byte by byte with a modulo.

The fix (same results, same cycle counts):
  - m_nextCommitCycle keeps the earliest readyCycle of the queued entries
    (updated at every insertion, recomputed after each commit, reset with
    the scheduler). commitReadyPipelines returns at once while m_cycle is
    below it; entries are still committed in the same order and at the
    same cycle as before.
  - flushPipelines: when only XGKICK is still active, it steps the cycle
    counter and PATH1 directly, which is what advanceOneCycle does when no
    pipeline entry is queued.
  - progressXgkick copies a qword with one memcpy when it does not wrap
    around VU memory.
  Tested: all 451 ps2xTest cases pass (the VU1 timing and XGKICK cases
  included). In the game (Linux, first level, the same route): from 1.43
  to 2.03 game frames per second (2.85 to 4.05 VBlanks per second),
  findings/19 s.9.

